from tn import *
def C(ch,*a,**k): TN(10,"science",ch,*a,**k)
C("solutions","Asha stirred sugar into her tea and saw it vanish. \"Solutions are everywhere in daily life,\" said her teacher.",
 ["A solution is a homogeneous mixture of two or more substances. Its components are the solute, present in the smaller amount, and the solvent, present in the larger amount. Solutions are classified by physical state (solid, liquid, gas), by the type of solvent such as aqueous solutions, and by the amount of solute as unsaturated, saturated and supersaturated, or concentrated and dilute.",
  "Solubility is the amount of solute that dissolves in a given amount of solvent at a fixed temperature, and it depends on temperature, pressure and the nature of solute and solvent. Concentration is expressed as mass percentage, volume percentage or other ways.",
  "Hydrated salts, such as copper sulphate and magnesium sulphate, contain water of crystallisation. Hygroscopic substances absorb moisture from the air and deliquescent substances absorb so much that they dissolve."],
 ["solute and solvent","saturated solution","solubility","mass percentage","water of crystallisation","deliquescence"],
 ("10 g of salt is dissolved in 90 g of water. Find the mass percentage of the solution.",["Mass of solution = 10 + 90 = 100 g.","Mass percentage = (10 / 100) x 100.","So it is 10 percent."]),
 ("The solvent is always the substance in the smaller amount.",["The solute is in the smaller amount.","The solvent is in the larger amount."]),
 ["A solution is homogeneous.","Solubility depends on temperature.","Mass % = solute / solution x 100.","Hydrated salts hold water of crystallisation."],
 ["m|The component in the larger amount is the|solvent|solute|precipitate|Like water in salt water.","t|A saturated solution cannot dissolve more solute.|T|At that temperature.","m|Copper sulphate crystals contain|water of crystallisation|oxygen|sodium|CuSO4 . 5H2O.","t|Sugar in tea is a solid-in-liquid solution.|T|Yes.","m|Substances that absorb moisture are|hygroscopic|volatile|magnetic|Like calcium chloride.","s|What does a solution have besides solvent?|solute|Solute.","m|Mass percentage is|solute mass / solution mass x 100|solvent / solute|solute x solvent|It uses total mass.","t|Solubility can change with temperature.|T|Yes."])
C("types-of-chemical-reactions","Raju saw iron turn red-brown after weeks in the rain. \"That is a chemical reaction called rusting,\" said his teacher.",
 ["Chemical reactions are of several types. In a combination reaction two or more substances form one product, in decomposition one substance breaks into simpler ones, in displacement a more reactive element replaces a less reactive one and in double displacement two compounds exchange ions. Reactions are also classed as reversible or irreversible.",
  "The rate of a chemical reaction is how fast the reactants change into products and depends on concentration, temperature, pressure, surface area and catalysts. In a reversible reaction the system reaches a state of equilibrium when the forward and backward rates are equal.",
  "The ionic product of water leads to the pH scale, pH = -log of the hydrogen ion concentration, with 7 neutral, below 7 acidic and above 7 basic. pH matters in everyday life, such as in the stomach, in teeth and in soil."],
 ["combination and decomposition","displacement","rate of reaction","equilibrium","pH scale","ionic product of water"],
 ("What is the pH of a neutral solution?",["pH is a scale from 0 to 14.","Pure water is neutral.","Its pH is 7."]),
 ("A pH of 3 means the solution is basic.",["Below 7 is acidic.","A pH of 3 is acidic."]),
 ["Reactions are of several types.","Rate depends on concentration, temperature and more.","Equilibrium has equal forward and backward rates.","pH 7 is neutral."],
 ["m|A reaction in which one substance breaks into simpler ones is|decomposition|combination|neutralisation|Heating limestone is an example.","t|Catalysts change reaction rate.|T|Yes.","m|A neutral solution has pH|7|1|14|Like pure water.","t|pH above 7 is acidic.|F|It is basic.","m|Reactions that can go both ways are|reversible|irreversible|physical|They reach equilibrium.","s|What scale measures acidity?|pH scale;pH|The pH scale.","m|Rusting is a|chemical reaction|physical change only|state change|New substance forms.","t|Temperature affects reaction rate.|T|Usually higher is faster."])
C("carbon-and-its-compounds","Meena saw a methane flame in the school lab. \"Carbon compounds burn well,\" said her teacher.",
 ["Organic compounds have special characteristics such as covalent bonding, low melting points and the ability to burn. They are classified by the carbon chain as open-chain or acyclic and closed-chain or cyclic compounds. Hydrocarbons are compounds of carbon and hydrogen only, and they are saturated alkanes or unsaturated alkenes and alkynes.",
  "Bromine water test: alkenes decolourise bromine water, but alkanes do not. A homologous series is a family of compounds with the same functional group where each member differs from the next by a CH2 group. Members have similar chemical properties and gradually changing physical properties.",
  "IUPAC nomenclature gives every organic compound a systematic name, with a root word for the number of carbons, a prefix and a suffix for the functional group. The rules for naming include choosing the longest chain, numbering it and naming the substituents."],
 ["organic compound","hydrocarbon","alkane and alkene","homologous series","IUPAC name","functional group"],
 ("What is the difference between two neighbouring members of a homologous series?",["Members are in a family.","They differ by one carbon and its hydrogens.","That is a CH2 group."]),
 ("Alkanes decolourise bromine water.",["Alkenes decolourise it.","Alkanes are saturated and do not."]),
 ["Hydrocarbons have only C and H.","A homologous series differs by CH2.","Alkenes decolourise bromine water.","IUPAC names organic compounds systematically."],
 ["m|Compounds of only carbon and hydrogen are|hydrocarbons|alcohols|acids|Like methane.","t|Alkenes decolourise bromine water.|T|They are unsaturated.","m|Neighbouring members of a homologous series differ by|CH2|CO2|OH|One carbon unit.","t|IUPAC stands for a system of naming.|T|Yes.","m|The simplest alkane is|methane|ethane|propane|CH4.","s|Name the group of compounds with C=C bonds.|alkenes;alkene|Alkenes.","m|A functional group decides the compound's|chemical properties|colour only|weight only|Its reactions.","t|Organic compounds are mostly covalent.|T|Yes."])
C("plant-anatomy-and-plant-physiology","Asha cut a stem and saw rings. \"Plants have an inner structure like us,\" said her teacher.",
 ["Plant tissues are grouped into three tissue systems. The dermal or epidermal system forms the outer covering, the ground tissue system forms the bulk of the plant and the vascular tissue system, with xylem and phloem, conducts water and food. Monocot and dicot roots and stems differ in the arrangement of these tissues.",
  "Plastids are cell structures, and the chloroplast, with chlorophyll, is where photosynthesis takes place. Photosynthesis uses light, carbon dioxide and water to make glucose and oxygen, and sunlight plays the key role. The light reaction happens in the grana and the dark reaction in the stroma of the chloroplast.",
  "The plant also respires, taking in oxygen to release energy from food, and the chapter compares photosynthesis and respiration."],
 ["epidermal system","ground tissue","vascular tissue","chloroplast","photosynthesis","respiration"],
 ("Which tissue system carries water in a plant?",["The vascular system conducts.","Xylem carries water.","So the vascular tissue system."]),
 ("Photosynthesis happens in the mitochondria.",["Photosynthesis takes place in chloroplasts.","Mitochondria do respiration."]),
 ["Three tissue systems: dermal, ground and vascular.","Xylem carries water, phloem carries food.","Chloroplast is the site of photosynthesis.","Photosynthesis gives glucose and oxygen."],
 ["m|The outer covering tissue system is the|epidermal|vascular|ground|Dermal.","t|Phloem carries food.|T|Yes.","m|Photosynthesis happens in the|chloroplast|nucleus|ribosome|Chlorophyll is there.","t|Oxygen is a product of photosynthesis.|T|Yes.","m|Xylem carries|water|hormones|blood|From roots up.","s|Which pigment traps light?|chlorophyll|Chlorophyll.","m|The vascular tissue system has|xylem and phloem|epidermis only|root hairs only|They conduct.","t|Dicot and monocot stems differ internally.|T|Yes."])
